# XSH newlocale()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Objects are inspected through nl_langinfo_l(); the
# contents of the POSIX locale are fixed by XBD 7.3, those of another locale are
# not, so such tests compare only against the POSIX code set (they are NOTINUSE
# without a second locale from pc_find_locale("UTF-8", 0), UNRESOLVED if the POSIX
# locale reported that code set too). The ENOMEM error and the may-fail EINVAL for
# a bad string pointer are not provoked. A base object is never used after a
# successful call that took it as base (its contents are then unspecified).

assertion  XSH.newlocale.001
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap07.html#7.2
class      required
since      2008
says       newlocale(LC_ALL_MASK, "C", (locale_t)0) and the same with "POSIX"
           return a handle other than (locale_t)0, and nl_langinfo_l() on it
           gives the POSIX day, month, format, RADIXCHAR and YESEXPR values
           and the POSIX code set.
test       t/001-posix.c

assertion  XSH.newlocale.002
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       Each of the six masks LC_CTYPE_MASK, LC_NUMERIC_MASK,
           LC_TIME_MASK, LC_COLLATE_MASK, LC_MONETARY_MASK and
           LC_MESSAGES_MASK alone, and the OR of all six, with "C" and base
           (locale_t)0, gives a usable handle (not (locale_t)0).
test       t/002-masks.c

assertion  XSH.newlocale.003
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       For a new object (base (locale_t)0) the categories not in the mask
           come from the POSIX locale: a mask of LC_CTYPE_MASK with another
           locale gives that locale's code set but POSIX time, numeric and
           messages items, and a mask of any one other single category with
           another locale gives the POSIX code set.
test       t/003-unrequested-posix.c

assertion  XSH.newlocale.004
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With a non-zero base object made from another locale, newlocale()
           with LC_CTYPE_MASK and "C" returns a handle (not (locale_t)0) whose
           code set is the POSIX one (the data replaces the base's), while the
           LC_TIME, LC_NUMERIC and LC_MESSAGES items of the result (day and
           month names, formats, RADIXCHAR, YESEXPR, NOEXPR) are the ones the
           base had (the categories not in the mask are kept). The base is not
           used afterwards.
test       t/004-modify-base.c

assertion  XSH.newlocale.005
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       With a name no locale has (and a valid mask) newlocale() returns
           (locale_t)0 and sets errno to ENOENT; if the system accepts the
           made-up name the test is UNRESOLVED.
test       t/005-enoent.c

assertion  XSH.newlocale.006
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2008
says       When newlocale() fails with a non-zero base, the base stays valid
           and unchanged: nl_langinfo_l() on it gives the same code set, day
           name, month name and RADIXCHAR as before the call.
test       t/006-failure-base.c

assertion  XSH.newlocale.007
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#ERRORS
class      disputed
since      2008
says       A mask with a bit outside LC_ALL_MASK (a bit that is no category)
           makes newlocale() return (locale_t)0 with errno EINVAL; if
           LC_ALL_MASK covers every bit of an int the test is NOTINUSE.
note       disputed (Q-135): the text allows implementation-defined mask values
           and does not say that LC_ALL_MASK includes them, so a bit outside
           LC_ALL_MASK may be a valid category. A FAIL is logged as INSPECT.
test       t/007-einval.c

assertion  XSH.newlocale.008
anchor     functions/newlocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap07.html#7.1
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.2
class      required
since      2008
says       newlocale(LC_ALL_MASK, "", (locale_t)0) takes the locale from the
           environment: with LC_ALL set to "POSIX" (LC_* and LANG naming a
           missing locale) and with LANG alone set to "POSIX", it returns a
           handle for the POSIX locale (POSIX day, month, RADIXCHAR and YESEXPR
           values and code set).
test       t/008-env.c
